    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as stiffness/spasms/ hyperlordosis, positive antibodies such as anti-GAD, EMG showing continuous motor-unit activity, response to benzodiazepines",
      "quote": "Stiff person syndrome (SPS) is a neurological disorder characterized by hypertonia and rigidity of the extremities and the trunk that leads to a restricted range of motion, slow voluntary movements, muscle hypertrophy and acquisition of abnormal postures such as lumbar hyperlordosis. Patients also develop painful muscle spasms that may be triggered by emotional upset, startle (tactile, auditory and visual stimuli) or by sudden movements generated by muscle contractions of adjacent muscles. Electromyography (EMG) shows a continuous motorunit activity, despite attempted relaxation. SPS has an estimated prevalence of 1–2 cases per million, and it is more frequent in women, with a mean age of onset of 45 years old. Although the pathogenesis is not well identi fied, it is considered autoimmune based on the presence of glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD) antibodies. Anti-GAD would cause an imbalance between the catecholaminergic excitatory and GAD inhibitory pathways, that control the activity of spinal cord motoneurons [1].",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
